27
00:02:35.137 --> 00:02:35.990
Part 1.

28
00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.190
Introduction and Why It Matters.

29
00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.595
A story: the Monday morning report.

30
00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:49.597
At 9:30 on the Monday after the Diwali weekend, checkout p99 jumped from 300 ms to 4 s.

31
00:02:49.877 --> 00:03:00.672
The cause was a BI dashboard: the finance team's weekly GMV report ran fifty count(distinct orderid)... group by week queries against the orders primary.

32
00:03:00.952 --> 00:03:03.469
Each one scanned 10 million rows (L3:

33
00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:10.619
5.4 s on PostgreSQL), and together they took the buffer cache and the CPUs away from checkout.

34
00:03:10.899 --> 00:03:12.883
The review found four more problems:

35
00:03:14.233 --> 00:03:17.561
Product images were served by catalog-service itself.

36
00:03:17.841 --> 00:03:27.355
They were stored as bytea rows and streamed through the JVM, so 40 % of the service's CPU and most of its egress went to bytes that never change.

37
00:03:27.635 --> 00:03:32.670
The CDN had been added the previous month, but its hit ratio was 55 %.

38
00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:42.870
Marketing push notifications appended utmsource, utmcampaign and fbclid to image URLs, and the CDN used the full URL as the cache key.

39
00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:43.961
L2:

40
00:03:44.241 --> 00:03:50.278
57.4 %, then 97.5 % once the key was normalised.

41
00:03:51.628 --> 00:04:04.257
A price cut went live at 00:00, but some customers saw the old price until 00:05, because product JSON was cached for 5 minutes and there was no purge.

42
00:04:04.537 --> 00:04:06.841
L2 reproduces it at a smaller scale:

43
00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:14.951
93 of 138 responses stale with a TTL alone, and 0 with purge or versioned URLs.

44
00:04:15.231 --> 00:04:19.924
The dark-store demand board undercounted evening orders by up to 30 %.

45
00:04:20.204 --> 00:04:28.993
It counted orders by the minute they arrived at the stream processor, not the minute they were placed, and riders' phones in basements delivered events minutes late.

46
00:04:29.273 --> 00:04:34.713
L4: processing-time windows were exact for 29.8 % of store-minutes.

47
00:04:36.063 --> 00:04:37.045
The fixes:

48
00:04:37.325 --> 00:04:39.245
Analytics moved off the OLTP path:

49
00:04:39.525 --> 00:04:44.858
CDC and nightly ELT into a Parquet lake, queried by a columnar engine.

50
00:04:45.138 --> 00:04:48.359
Blobs moved to object storage with presigned uploads.

51
00:04:48.639 --> 00:04:57.215
The CDN got normalised keys and coalescing, plus versioned URLs for static assets, purge for prices, and stale-if-error.

52
00:04:57.495 --> 00:05:02.167
The demand board moved to event-time windows with a watermark and allowed lateness.

53
00:05:03.517 --> 00:05:04.200
Quote:

54
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.144
The thesis of this chapter.

55
00:05:06.424 --> 00:05:08.408
Different data has different physics.

56
00:05:08.688 --> 00:05:11.547
Transactions want rows, indexes and locks.

57
00:05:11.827 --> 00:05:14.749
Analytics want columns, compression and full scans.

58
00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:17.888
Blobs want cheap, durable, flat namespaces.

59
00:05:18.168 --> 00:05:20.301
Static bytes want to live near users.

60
00:05:20.581 --> 00:05:31.461
Scaling the data layer is mostly about putting each kind of data in the store built for it, and moving it between stores with pipelines that are replayable, idempotent and honest about time.

61
00:05:32.811 --> 00:05:35.115
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

