137
00:09:49.894 --> 00:09:51.345
File in a hierarchy.

138
00:09:51.625 --> 00:09:57.875
Object storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, SeaweedFS, Ceph RGW):

139
00:09:58.155 --> 00:10:01.547
Immutable object in a flat bucket, key + metadata.

140
00:10:01.827 --> 00:10:02.702
Access.

141
00:10:02.982 --> 00:10:04.689
Block storage (EBS):

142
00:10:04.969 --> 00:10:06.398
Mounted by one host.

143
00:10:06.678 --> 00:10:09.217
File storage (EFS or NFS):

144
00:10:09.497 --> 00:10:10.670
Mounted by many.

145
00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:17.201
Object storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, SeaweedFS, Ceph RGW):

146
00:10:17.481 --> 00:10:21.342
HTTP API (S3 is the de facto standard).

147
00:10:21.622 --> 00:10:22.390
Scale.

148
00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:24.377
Block storage (EBS):

149
00:10:24.657 --> 00:10:26.022
TBs per volume.

150
00:10:26.302 --> 00:10:28.841
File storage (EFS or NFS):

151
00:10:29.121 --> 00:10:29.953
Large.

152
00:10:30.233 --> 00:10:36.483
Object storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, SeaweedFS, Ceph RGW):

153
00:10:36.763 --> 00:10:39.131
Effectively unlimited objects and bytes.

154
00:10:39.411 --> 00:10:40.371
Durability.

155
00:10:40.651 --> 00:10:42.358
Block storage (EBS):

156
00:10:42.638 --> 00:10:44.046
Replicated in a zone.

157
00:10:44.326 --> 00:10:46.865
File storage (EFS or NFS):

158
00:10:47.145 --> 00:10:48.041
Regional.

159
00:10:48.321 --> 00:10:54.571
Object storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, SeaweedFS, Ceph RGW):

160
00:10:54.851 --> 00:10:58.969
11 nines (S3 Standard), erasure-coded across zones.

161
00:10:59.249 --> 00:10:59.995
Use.

162
00:11:00.275 --> 00:11:01.982
Block storage (EBS):

163
00:11:02.262 --> 00:11:03.393
Databases.

164
00:11:03.673 --> 00:11:06.211
File storage (EFS or NFS):

165
00:11:06.491 --> 00:11:08.262
Shared config, legacy apps.

166
00:11:08.542 --> 00:11:14.793
Object storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, SeaweedFS, Ceph RGW):

167
00:11:15.073 --> 00:11:17.441
Media, backups, logs, data lakes.

168
00:11:18.791 --> 00:11:21.052
The rules the grocery platform follows:

169
00:11:21.332 --> 00:11:23.465
Don't put blobs in the OLTP database.

170
00:11:23.745 --> 00:11:26.668
It bloats backups, replication and the buffer cache.

171
00:11:26.948 --> 00:11:31.769
Store the object key and metadata in the DB and the bytes in object storage.

172
00:11:32.049 --> 00:11:34.097
Don't proxy bytes through app servers.

173
00:11:34.377 --> 00:11:45.684
Use presigned URLs: the service authorises the action and signs a URL scoped to one method, one key and a short expiry, and the client talks directly to storage.

174
00:11:45.964 --> 00:11:49.079
L1: the presigned PUT and GET succeeded (200).

175
00:11:50.429 --> 00:11:57.746
The same URL with the key changed (rider-78) got 403, because the signature covers the path.

176
00:11:58.026 --> 00:12:04.490
An expired URL got 403, and an unsigned GET of a private object got 403.

177
00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:15.266
Multipart upload for large objects (S3 recommends it above ~100 MB; parts are 5 MB, 5 GB, up to 10,000 of them):

178
00:12:15.546 --> 00:12:19.258
Parts upload in parallel, and a failed part is retried alone.

179
00:12:19.538 --> 00:12:27.111
L1 sent 44 MB for a 40 MB object with one failure, where a single PUT would have resent all 40.

180
00:12:28.461 --> 00:12:32.771
Clean up incomplete uploads with a lifecycle rule, or they're billed forever.

181
00:12:33.051 --> 00:12:33.861
Keys:

182
00:12:34.141 --> 00:12:41.565
Design for listing (products/sku/... exports/date/...) and for lifecycle rules by prefix.

183
00:12:41.845 --> 00:12:49.973
Content-addressed keys (blobs or sha256/hash) give deduplication and immutability for free (L1:

184
00:12:50.253 --> 00:12:51.896
3 uploads, 1 object).

185
00:12:52.176 --> 00:13:01.243
Old advice about random prefixes for throughput is obsolete on S3: each prefix scales to thousands of requests per second automatically.

186
00:13:02.593 --> 00:13:03.617
Consistency:

187
00:13:03.897 --> 00:13:10.467
S3 has been strongly consistent for reads after writes and for lists since December 2020.

188
00:13:10.747 --> 00:13:14.054
Many S3-compatible stores are too, but check yours.

189
00:13:14.334 --> 00:13:29.822
Lifecycle and storage classes: transition old invoices to infrequent-access or archive tiers, expire temporary uploads, version buckets that hold critical data, and use Object Lock (WORM) for compliance (Ch 36).

190
00:13:30.102 --> 00:13:35.371
ETags of multipart objects aren't content MD5s (L1:...-10).

191
00:13:35.651 --> 00:13:39.619
Verify integrity with your own checksum or the newer checksum headers.

192
00:13:40.969 --> 00:13:42.484
CDN fundamentals.

193
00:13:42.764 --> 00:13:50.060
A CDN is a network of edge caches (points of presence) that serve content close to users and shield the origin.

194
00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:51.556
Pull versus push:

195
00:13:52.906 --> 00:13:54.250
Here is a quick table:

196
00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.407
Pull (origin pull), and Push.

197
00:13:56.687 --> 00:13:57.349
How.

198
00:13:57.629 --> 00:13:59.015
Pull (origin pull):

199
00:13:59.295 --> 00:14:03.114
The edge fetches from the origin on a miss and caches per the headers.

200
00:14:03.394 --> 00:14:04.055
Push:

201
00:14:04.335 --> 00:14:07.578
You upload content to the CDN's storage ahead of time.

202
00:14:07.858 --> 00:14:08.690
Good for.

203
00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:10.357
Pull (origin pull):

204
00:14:10.637 --> 00:14:13.261
Most web content, APIs, images.

205
00:14:13.541 --> 00:14:14.202
Push:

206
00:14:14.482 --> 00:14:17.874
Large, predictable releases (app bundles, videos).

207
00:14:18.154 --> 00:14:18.879
Risk.

208
00:14:19.159 --> 00:14:20.546
Pull (origin pull):

209
00:14:20.826 --> 00:14:24.197
The first request per edge is a miss; herds on expiry.

210
00:14:24.477 --> 00:14:25.138
Push:

211
00:14:25.418 --> 00:14:26.719
You manage what's there.

212
00:14:28.069 --> 00:14:30.032
Cache headers the origin controls:

213
00:14:30.312 --> 00:14:54.696
Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable for versioned assets. max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=30, stale-if-error=86400 for semi-dynamic JSON. s-maxage sets the shared-cache TTL separately from browsers. private or no-store for personalised responses.

214
00:14:56.046 --> 00:14:59.395
Vary for content negotiation (keep it minimal; Vary:

215
00:14:59.675 --> 00:15:01.915
User-Agent destroys hit ratios).

216
00:15:02.195 --> 00:15:05.651
Cache keys: by default, the full URL plus some headers.

217
00:15:05.931 --> 00:15:17.622
Normalise them: drop tracking params (utm, fbclid, gclid);. sort query params;. lower-case where safe;. bucket device classes instead of varying on the raw User-Agent.

218
00:15:18.972 --> 00:15:19.783
L2:

219
00:15:20.063 --> 00:15:24.436
20,000 image requests with 40 % carrying tracking params.

220
00:15:24.716 --> 00:15:36.855
A full-URL key gave 57.4 % hits and 406 MB of origin egress; a normalised key gave 97.5 % and 23 MB.

221
00:15:37.135 --> 00:15:45.988
Request coalescing (collapsed forwarding): when many requests for the same key miss at once, send one to the origin and make the rest wait for it.

222
00:15:46.268 --> 00:15:47.079
L2:

223
00:15:47.359 --> 00:15:51.497
400, then 1 origin requests when a hot product expired.

224
00:15:51.777 --> 00:15:58.497
Most CDNs do this per POP, and origin shield or tiered caching extends it across POPs.

225
00:15:59.847 --> 00:16:00.957
Invalidation.

226
00:16:01.237 --> 00:16:04.117
There are three strategies, and you usually combine them:

227
00:16:05.467 --> 00:16:06.811
Here is a quick table:

228
00:16:07.091 --> 00:16:09.736
Strategy, and How, and Staleness, and Cost.

229
00:16:10.016 --> 00:16:11.211
TTL only.

230
00:16:11.491 --> 00:16:12.152
How:

231
00:16:12.432 --> 00:16:13.712
Wait for max-age.

232
00:16:13.992 --> 00:16:14.888
