356
00:21:35.477 --> 00:21:41.365
Yes (merge-on-read or copy-on-write), essential for GDPR or DPDP deletes.

357
00:21:42.715 --> 00:21:43.440
Cost.

358
00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:44.829
Data warehouse:

359
00:21:45.109 --> 00:21:47.520
Compute + storage coupled (less so now).

360
00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:48.739
Data lake:

361
00:21:49.019 --> 00:21:50.192
Cheapest storage.

362
00:21:50.472 --> 00:21:51.411
Lakehouse:

363
00:21:51.691 --> 00:21:53.845
Cheap storage + choice of compute.

364
00:21:55.195 --> 00:22:01.041
Table formats add a metadata layer (manifests, snapshots) over immutable Parquet files.

365
00:22:01.321 --> 00:22:12.563
That gives atomic commits (a new snapshot pointer), concurrent writers, schema and partition evolution, time travel (revenue as of last Tuesday's snapshot) and efficient deletes.

366
00:22:12.843 --> 00:22:18.795
L3's hive-style dt= directories are the pre-table-format version of partitioning.

367
00:22:19.075 --> 00:22:23.449
They work, but renames aren't atomic and every reader must list directories.

368
00:22:23.729 --> 00:22:25.094
The grocery platform:

369
00:22:25.374 --> 00:22:38.451
Iceberg tables on object storage, written by CDC (Debezium, then Kafka, then Flink or Spark sink) and nightly ELT, and queried by Trino for BI and by Spark for ML features.

370
00:22:38.731 --> 00:22:44.171
A star schema (fact orderline, dimensions store, sku, customer, date) in the gold layer.

371
00:22:45.521 --> 00:22:47.207
ETL versus ELT.

372
00:22:47.487 --> 00:22:48.255
ETL:

373
00:22:48.535 --> 00:22:52.929
Extract, then Transform (in a separate engine), then Load the cleaned result.

374
00:22:53.209 --> 00:22:57.049
Classic warehousing, where transformations happen before the warehouse.

375
00:22:57.329 --> 00:22:57.991
ELT:

376
00:22:58.271 --> 00:23:04.927
Extract, then Load raw data into the lake or warehouse, then Transform inside it with SQL (dbt).

377
00:23:05.207 --> 00:23:09.473
Raw data is kept, so transforms can be re-run when logic changes.

378
00:23:09.753 --> 00:23:16.623
Medallion layers: bronze: raw, append-only, as received;. silver: cleaned, deduplicated, conformed;.

379
00:23:17.973 --> 00:23:20.255
gold: business aggregates and marts.

380
00:23:20.535 --> 00:23:38.946
L3's pipeline is ELT in miniature: extract with COPY (754 MB CSV, 9.2 s), then load and transform in DuckDB into partitioned Parquet with a derived local-date column (58 MB, 7.0 s).

381
00:23:39.226 --> 00:23:40.570
Extraction options:

382
00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:45.330
Batch COPY/snapshot: simple, but loads the source; use a replica.

383
00:23:45.610 --> 00:23:49.429
Incremental by updatedat: misses deletes and needs indexes.

384
00:23:50.779 --> 00:23:57.456
CDC from the WAL (Debezium, Ch 13): complete, low latency, captures deletes.

385
00:23:57.736 --> 00:23:59.059
The platform standard.

386
00:24:00.409 --> 00:24:01.603
Batch processing.

387
00:24:01.883 --> 00:24:11.526
Batch jobs process a bounded dataset: nightly settlement, invoice generation, loyalty expiry, reconciliation, ML feature backfills.

388
00:24:11.806 --> 00:24:13.193
Spring Batch's model:

389
00:24:13.473 --> 00:24:14.646
Job, then Steps.

390
00:24:14.926 --> 00:24:22.542
A step is either chunk-oriented (ItemReader, then ItemProcessor, then ItemWriter with a commit interval) or a tasklet.

391
00:24:22.822 --> 00:24:30.630
A JobRepository stores execution state and the ExecutionContext (the checkpoint, for example, the last key read).

392
00:24:30.910 --> 00:24:33.811
A failed job restarts from the last committed chunk.

393
00:24:34.091 --> 00:24:39.915
Skip or retry policies handle bad records, and partitioning or remote chunking scale out.

394
00:24:41.265 --> 00:24:49.372
The correctness rule, from L4 Part A: the checkpoint and the chunk's output must commit atomically, or the output must be idempotent.

395
00:24:50.722 --> 00:24:52.066
Here is a quick table:

396
00:24:52.346 --> 00:24:55.183
Restart strategy, and Result after one crash.

397
00:24:55.463 --> 00:24:57.917
Output committed, crash before the checkpoint:

398
00:24:58.197 --> 00:25:05.322
Chunk 60 re-processed, 200 (day, store) rows wrong, 5.37 crore rupees overcounted.

399
00:25:05.602 --> 00:25:08.461
Checkpoint in the same transaction as the output:

400
00:25:08.741 --> 00:25:09.594
Exact.

401
00:25:09.874 --> 00:25:13.949
Recompute and overwrite the output partition in one transaction:

402
00:25:14.229 --> 00:25:15.487
Exact, simplest.

403
00:25:15.767 --> 00:25:20.141
Re-read everything (20M rows for 10M), still fastest here.

404
00:25:21.491 --> 00:25:29.363
In lakehouse terms, overwrite means INSERT OVERWRITE partition dt=... or an Iceberg replacePartitions commit.

405
00:25:29.643 --> 00:25:34.656
Recomputing a day and atomically replacing it is the most robust batch pattern.

406
00:25:36.006 --> 00:25:37.243
Stream processing.

407
00:25:37.523 --> 00:25:40.745
Streaming processes an unbounded log continuously:

408
00:25:41.025 --> 00:25:47.233
Kafka Streams (a library inside your service), Apache Flink (a cluster), Spark Structured Streaming.

409
00:25:47.513 --> 00:25:48.643
The core concepts:

410
00:25:48.923 --> 00:25:51.035
Event time versus processing time:

411
00:25:51.315 --> 00:25:53.982
Event time is when it happened on the rider's phone.

412
00:25:54.262 --> 00:25:56.886
Processing time is when the processor saw it.

413
00:25:57.166 --> 00:25:59.705
They differ by seconds to minutes (L4:

414
00:25:59.985 --> 00:26:04.102
10 % of events more than 5 s late, 2 % more than 2 min).

415
00:26:05.452 --> 00:26:11.383
Windows: tumbling (fixed, non-overlapping: orders per minute);. hopping or sliding (overlapping:

416
00:26:11.663 --> 00:26:16.783
5-min demand every minute);. session (gap-based: a customer's browsing session).

417
00:26:17.063 --> 00:26:22.119
Watermark: the processor's assertion that no events older than t are still expected.

418
00:26:22.399 --> 00:26:25.620
Commonly max event time seen minus bounded delay.

419
00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:29.441
A window's result is emitted when the watermark passes its end.

420
00:26:30.791 --> 00:26:37.319
Allowed lateness: keep window state after firing, and emit updated results for late events up to a limit.

421
00:26:37.599 --> 00:26:39.477
Downstream must accept upserts.

422
00:26:39.757 --> 00:26:53.538
State and checkpoints: window state lives in a local store (RocksDB), checkpointed together with input offsets (Flink snapshots, Kafka Streams changelog topics), which gives exactly-once state semantics.

