464
00:28:25.326 --> 00:28:30.574
The demand board takes the last row: fast first results, corrected as stragglers arrive.

465
00:28:31.924 --> 00:28:33.311
Lambda versus Kappa.

466
00:28:33.591 --> 00:28:42.636
Lambda runs a batch layer (complete, recomputed from the master dataset) and a speed layer (streaming, approximate, recent), merged at query time.

467
00:28:42.916 --> 00:28:45.199
It's robust: batch corrects the stream.

468
00:28:45.479 --> 00:28:49.084
But it has two codebases for the same logic that drift apart.

469
00:28:49.364 --> 00:28:51.263
Kappa has one streaming codebase.

470
00:28:51.543 --> 00:29:03.553
Reprocessing means replaying the log (Kafka with long retention or tiered storage, or the lake's bronze layer) through a new version of the job, into a new output, and then switching readers over.

471
00:29:04.903 --> 00:29:08.573
It needs a replayable log and a job that can catch up fast.

472
00:29:08.853 --> 00:29:20.202
L4's toy job replayed the evening at ~4.5M events per second on one core; real jobs with state and I or O are much slower, so plan catch-up capacity.

473
00:29:20.482 --> 00:29:33.325
In practice, 2026: most platforms run kappa-style streaming for fresh metrics, plus a lakehouse whose tables are the durable history and can be recomputed by batch (a lakehouse lambda).

474
00:29:33.605 --> 00:29:40.559
Unified engines (Flink, Spark) share code between batch and streaming, which removes the classic lambda drift.

475
00:29:40.839 --> 00:29:42.205
The grocery platform:

476
00:29:43.555 --> 00:29:48.653
Streaming: the demand board, surge, fraud and live ETAs (Flink or Kafka Streams).

477
00:29:48.933 --> 00:29:54.032
Batch: finance, settlement and ML training (Spark or Spring Batch on Iceberg).

478
00:29:54.312 --> 00:30:00.093
Where both exist, the batch figure is the source of truth, and the streaming figure is labelled provisional.

