551
00:33:27.835 --> 00:33:28.475
1.

552
00:33:28.755 --> 00:33:30.163
Context and Problem.

553
00:33:30.443 --> 00:33:32.961
The Monday incident (§1.1):

554
00:33:33.241 --> 00:33:44.825
BI on the OLTP primary, blobs in PostgreSQL behind the JVM, a 55 % CDN hit ratio, stale prices, and processing-time demand counts.

555
00:33:45.105 --> 00:33:45.809
2.

556
00:33:46.089 --> 00:33:47.433
Goals or Non-Goals.

557
00:33:47.713 --> 00:34:03.201
Goals: no analytical load on OLTP primaries; a CDN hit ratio 95 % for media; price changes visible within 10 s; demand metrics exact within the lateness bound; finance numbers reproducible from raw data.

558
00:34:03.481 --> 00:34:08.601
Non-goals: real-time finance (T+1 is fine); a self-built CDN.

559
00:34:08.881 --> 00:34:09.627
3.

560
00:34:09.907 --> 00:34:10.867
Requirements.

561
00:34:12.217 --> 00:34:13.561
Here is a quick table:

562
00:34:13.841 --> 00:34:15.313
ID, and Requirement.

563
00:34:15.593 --> 00:34:16.532
FR-1:

564
00:34:16.812 --> 00:34:25.047
Uploads go directly to object storage with presigned URLs ( at most 5 min expiry, one key, content type fixed).

565
00:34:25.327 --> 00:34:26.265
FR-2:

566
00:34:26.545 --> 00:34:34.887
Every static asset URL is content-versioned; product or price JSON is tagged with surrogate keys and purged on change.

567
00:34:35.167 --> 00:34:36.148
FR-3:

568
00:34:36.428 --> 00:34:42.124
BI and finance query only the lakehouse; OLTP read access for analysts is revoked.

569
00:34:42.404 --> 00:34:43.449
NFR-1:

570
00:34:43.729 --> 00:34:50.833
Media hit ratio ≥ 95 %; origin egress at most 10 % of edge egress.

571
00:34:51.113 --> 00:34:52.159
NFR-2:

572
00:34:52.439 --> 00:35:00.161
Lakehouse freshness: silver at most 15 min behind OLTP (CDC); gold daily by 03:00.

573
00:35:00.441 --> 00:35:01.508
NFR-3:

574
00:35:01.788 --> 00:35:06.460
Batch jobs restartable with exact results (tested with injected crashes).

575
00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:07.828
NFR-4:

576
00:35:08.108 --> 00:35:13.463
Demand board first result at most 10 s after the minute ends; exact after 30 min.

577
00:35:14.813 --> 00:35:15.517
4.

578
00:35:15.797 --> 00:35:17.077
Capacity estimates.

579
00:35:18.427 --> 00:35:19.771
Here is a quick table:

580
00:35:20.051 --> 00:35:22.355
Item, and Calculation, and Result.

581
00:35:22.635 --> 00:35:23.467
Media.

582
00:35:23.747 --> 00:35:24.813
Calculation:

583
00:35:25.093 --> 00:35:31.323
400K SKUs times 6 images times 3 sizes times 150 KB.

584
00:35:31.603 --> 00:35:40.435
Result: ~1.1 TB, plus PoD photos 60K per day times 300 KB about 18 GB per day.

585
00:35:40.715 --> 00:35:41.973
CDN egress.

586
00:35:42.253 --> 00:35:43.320
Calculation:

587
00:35:43.600 --> 00:35:47.867
10M app sessions per day times 40 images times 60 KB.

588
00:35:48.147 --> 00:35:56.000
Result: ~24 TB per day at the edge; at most 1.2 TB per day at the origin with a 95 % hit ratio.

589
00:35:57.280 --> 00:35:58.240
Order lines.

590
00:35:58.520 --> 00:35:59.587
Calculation:

591
00:35:59.867 --> 00:36:02.811
1.2M orders per day times 5 lines.

592
00:36:03.091 --> 00:36:03.901
Result:

593
00:36:04.181 --> 00:36:08.917
6M rows per day, ~35 MB per day as Parquet (L3:

594
00:36:09.197 --> 00:36:11.395
58 MB per 10M rows).

595
00:36:11.675 --> 00:36:13.189
3 years of order lines.

596
00:36:13.469 --> 00:36:14.536
Calculation:

597
00:36:14.816 --> 00:36:16.352
6.6B rows.

598
00:36:16.632 --> 00:36:24.312
Result: ~40 GB Parquet, trivial for a lakehouse; would be ~900 GB in PostgreSQL.

599
00:36:24.592 --> 00:36:25.637
Stream state.

600
00:36:25.917 --> 00:36:26.984
Calculation:

601
00:36:27.264 --> 00:36:32.811
900 stores times 31 open minutes (30 min lateness) times a few counters.

602
00:36:33.091 --> 00:36:33.901
Result:

603
00:36:34.181 --> 00:36:36.123
Kilobytes per store: tiny.

604
00:36:37.473 --> 00:36:38.241
5.

605
00:36:38.521 --> 00:36:39.715
Proposed design.

606
00:36:39.995 --> 00:36:58.897
Object storage: private buckets per data class (media, documents, exports), SSE-KMS encryption, lifecycle rules (temp uploads 1 day, incomplete multipart 7 days, invoices, then IA after 90 days), versioning on documents, Object Lock for tax invoices.

607
00:36:59.177 --> 00:37:00.115
CDN:

608
00:37:00.395 --> 00:37:15.265
Cache policy per path: /img/ gets a normalised key and 1 year immutable. /api or catalog/ gets 60 s + SWR 30 s + SIE 24 h + surrogate keys.

609
00:37:16.615 --> 00:37:22.759
Origin shield. catalog-service purges by SKU tag on price or stock-status change.

610
00:37:23.039 --> 00:37:23.977
Lakehouse:

611
00:37:24.257 --> 00:37:29.505
Debezium CDC, then Kafka, then a Flink sink writes bronze Iceberg tables.

612
00:37:29.785 --> 00:37:36.569
Flink or Spark jobs build silver (dedup by event id, Ch 20) and gold (star schema) tables.

613
00:37:36.849 --> 00:37:38.513
Partitioned by local date.

614
00:37:38.793 --> 00:37:43.508
Trino for BI, with DuckDB allowed for local analysis of extracts.

615
00:37:44.858 --> 00:37:45.626
Batch:

616
00:37:45.906 --> 00:37:52.391
Spring Batch jobs for settlement and invoices, with chunk checkpoints in the job's own DB transaction.

617
00:37:52.671 --> 00:37:56.042
Spark jobs for gold tables use partition overwrite.

618
00:37:56.322 --> 00:37:57.154
Streaming:

619
00:37:57.434 --> 00:38:07.909
Kafka Streams for the demand board (per-store tumbling 1-min windows, grace 30 min, suppress-until-final for the finance-facing view), and Flink for surge and fraud.

620
