941
01:04:08.432 --> 01:04:10.459
Numbers wrong, job succeeded.

942
01:04:10.739 --> 01:04:11.507
Cause:

943
01:04:11.787 --> 01:04:14.411
Checkpoint not atomic with output (L4).

944
01:04:14.691 --> 01:04:15.693
Mitigation:

945
01:04:15.973 --> 01:04:20.624
Same-transaction checkpoint or partition overwrite; reconciliation checks.

946
01:04:21.974 --> 01:04:23.147
Stream undercounts.

947
01:04:23.427 --> 01:04:24.238
Symptom:

948
01:04:24.518 --> 01:04:25.883
Metrics low at peak.

949
01:04:26.163 --> 01:04:26.931
Cause:

950
01:04:27.211 --> 01:04:30.433
Processing-time windows, watermark too tight (L4).

951
01:04:30.713 --> 01:04:31.715
Mitigation:

952
01:04:31.995 --> 01:04:34.747
Event time, lateness, dropped-event metrics.

953
01:04:35.027 --> 01:04:36.670
Schema drift in pipelines.

954
01:04:36.950 --> 01:04:37.761
Symptom:

955
01:04:38.041 --> 01:04:40.494
Jobs fail or silently null columns.

956
01:04:40.774 --> 01:04:41.542
Cause:

957
01:04:41.822 --> 01:04:43.315
Upstream changed a field.

958
01:04:43.595 --> 01:04:44.598
Mitigation:

959
01:04:44.878 --> 01:04:50.894
Schema registry, contracts (Ch 28), fail-fast validation in bronze, then silver.

960
01:04:52.244 --> 01:04:53.332
Observability.

961
01:04:53.612 --> 01:05:06.711
CDN: hit ratio by path and POP, origin requests and egress, 5xx at the edge versus the origin, purge latency, stale responses served (SIE or SWR).

962
01:05:06.991 --> 01:05:19.748
Object storage: request rates, 4xx (bad signatures, expiries), 5xx or throttling (503 SlowDown), bytes by bucket and class, incomplete multipart bytes.

963
01:05:20.028 --> 01:05:32.423
Lakehouse: per-table freshness (data as of), rows per partition versus expected;. file count and average file size;. query queue time and bytes scanned per query (the main cost driver).

964
01:05:33.773 --> 01:05:40.877
Batch: duration trend, rows read or written, restarts and chunks redone, reconciliation diffs versus source.

965
01:05:41.157 --> 01:05:50.095
Streaming: consumer lag (in time), watermark lag, late and dropped events, updates emitted, checkpoint duration and size, state store size.

966
01:05:51.445 --> 01:05:52.384
Security.

967
01:05:52.664 --> 01:05:54.413
Buckets are private by default.

968
01:05:54.693 --> 01:05:57.061
Block public access at the account level.

969
01:05:57.341 --> 01:06:02.717
The CDN reads through an origin access identity or signed origin requests.

970
01:06:02.997 --> 01:06:04.811
Presigned URLs for users.

971
01:06:05.091 --> 01:06:07.843
Scope IAM per service and per prefix.

972
01:06:08.123 --> 01:06:09.019
Encryption:

973
01:06:09.299 --> 01:06:17.896
SSE-KMS at rest, TLS in transit, and per-tenant keys where contracts require it (Ch 23, Ch 36).

974
01:06:18.176 --> 01:06:19.477
PII in the lake:

975
01:06:20.827 --> 01:06:26.822
Pseudonymise customer identifiers in silver or gold, and keep the mapping in a restricted table.

976
01:06:27.102 --> 01:06:29.555
Column-level access in the query engine.

977
01:06:29.835 --> 01:06:39.777
Deletes for DPDP or GDPR require a table format that supports row deletes (Iceberg or Delta), which plain Parquet directories don't.

978
01:06:40.057 --> 01:06:45.390
CDN: never cache personalised responses (the cache key doesn't include the user).

979
01:06:45.670 --> 01:06:53.542
Mark them private, no-store at the origin, because a single misconfigured public header can leak one customer's cart to another.

980
01:06:54.892 --> 01:07:03.489
Presigned upload validation: after upload, verify size and content type, and scan for malware, before the object is referenced.

981
01:07:04.839 --> 01:07:06.503
Performance and tuning knobs.

982
01:07:06.783 --> 01:07:08.127
Here is a quick table:

983
01:07:08.407 --> 01:07:09.623
Knob, and Guidance.

984
01:07:09.903 --> 01:07:11.418
Multipart part size:

985
01:07:11.698 --> 01:07:16.541
8 to 64 MB; parallelism 4 to 16; retries per part.

986
01:07:16.821 --> 01:07:17.887
Cache-Control:

987
01:07:18.167 --> 01:07:19.277
Versioned assets:

988
01:07:19.557 --> 01:07:25.402
1 year immutable; JSON: short max-age + stale-while-revalidate + stale-if-error.

989
01:07:25.682 --> 01:07:26.578
Cache key:

990
01:07:26.858 --> 01:07:31.701
Allow-list of query params; ignore tracking params and cookies on static paths.

991
01:07:31.981 --> 01:07:39.959
Parquet row group size: ~128 MB (fewer, larger row groups scan faster; smaller ones prune better).

992
01:07:40.239 --> 01:07:41.199
Partitioning:

993
01:07:41.479 --> 01:07:49.501
By date (the most common filter), not by high-cardinality columns; target files of 128 MB, 1 GB.

994
01:07:49.781 --> 01:07:51.338
Sort order or clustering:

995
01:07:51.618 --> 01:07:57.271
Sort within partitions by common filters (storeid) to make min or max pruning effective.

996
01:07:58.621 --> 01:07:59.880
Batch chunk size:

997
01:08:00.160 --> 01:08:05.963
1,000 to 100,000; larger = fewer commits, more redo on restart.

998
01:08:06.243 --> 01:08:07.565
Watermark or grace:

999
01:08:07.845 --> 01:08:14.864
From the measured delay distribution (p99 or p99.9 of arrival delay), not a guess.

1000
01:08:15.144 --> 01:08:16.189
Stream state:

1001
01:08:16.469 --> 01:08:20.672
TTL on state; RocksDB tuning; incremental checkpoints.

1002
01:08:22.022 --> 01:08:22.747
Cost.

1003
01:08:23.027 --> 01:08:25.289
Egress is often the biggest line item.

1004
01:08:25.569 --> 01:08:37.217
CDN egress is cheaper than origin egress, and a hit ratio from 57 % to 97.5 % cut origin egress by 94 % in L2.

1005
01:08:37.497 --> 01:08:44.345
Storage classes: infrequent-access and archive tiers cost a fraction of standard, but charge for retrieval.

1006
01:08:44.625 --> 01:08:46.481
Model the access pattern first.

1007
01:08:46.761 --> 01:08:53.310
Lakehouse compute: engines like BigQuery, Athena and Trino on cloud storage bill by bytes scanned.

1008
01:08:53.590 --> 01:09:02.017
Columnar formats, partition pruning and precomputed columns (L3's dt) are cost controls as much as performance ones.

1009
01:09:03.367 --> 01:09:04.881
Streaming is always-on.

1010
01:09:05.161 --> 01:09:07.273
Only stream what needs seconds-freshness.

1011
01:09:07.553 --> 01:09:10.220
Most reports are fine T+1 in batch.

