01:12:32.112 --> 01:12:32.987
Part 8.

1060
01:12:33.267 --> 01:12:34.611
Interview Q and A.

1061
01:12:35.861 --> 01:12:36.693
Beginner.

1062
01:12:36.973 --> 01:12:37.997
Question 1.

1063
01:12:38.277 --> 01:12:41.861
Why store images in object storage instead of the database?

1064
01:12:42.141 --> 01:12:43.506
Thirty-second answer:

1065
01:12:43.786 --> 01:12:56.479
Object storage is built for large, immutable blobs: it's cheap per GB, extremely durable, scales without limits, and serves bytes over HTTP, directly or through a CDN.

1066
01:12:56.759 --> 01:13:04.269
Blobs in the database bloat backups, replication and the buffer cache, and every download goes through your application servers.

1067
01:13:04.549 --> 01:13:08.858
Store the key and metadata in the DB and the bytes in object storage.

1068
01:13:10.208 --> 01:13:11.211
Question 2.

1069
01:13:11.491 --> 01:13:13.667
What is a CDN and when does it help?

1070
01:13:13.947 --> 01:13:15.312
Thirty-second answer:

1071
01:13:15.592 --> 01:13:26.280
A network of caches close to users that serve copies of content (images, scripts, videos, cacheable API responses) so requests don't travel to the origin.

1072
01:13:26.560 --> 01:13:33.131
It helps with latency (nearby), origin load (offload) and resilience (stale content during outages).

1073
01:13:33.411 --> 01:13:37.827
It helps most for content that many users request and that changes rarely.

1074
01:13:39.177 --> 01:13:40.222
Question 3.

1075
01:13:40.502 --> 01:13:42.913
What is the difference between OLTP and OLAP?

1076
01:13:43.193 --> 01:13:44.558
Thirty-second answer:

1077
01:13:44.838 --> 01:13:52.049
OLTP (online transaction processing) serves many small reads and writes by key: placing an order, updating stock.

1078
01:13:52.329 --> 01:13:54.462
It uses row storage and indexes.

1079
01:13:54.742 --> 01:14:02.422
OLAP (online analytical processing) runs large scans and aggregations over history: revenue by category by week.

1080
01:14:02.702 --> 01:14:06.073
It uses columnar, compressed storage and parallel scans.

1081
01:14:07.423 --> 01:14:08.489
Question 4.

1082
01:14:08.769 --> 01:14:11.585
What is the difference between batch and stream processing?

1083
01:14:11.865 --> 01:14:13.231
Thirty-second answer:

1084
01:14:13.511 --> 01:14:19.249
Batch processes a bounded dataset on a schedule: last night's orders, all at once, exactly.

1085
01:14:19.529 --> 01:14:25.823
Stream processing handles an unbounded flow of events continuously, producing results within seconds.

1086
01:14:26.103 --> 01:14:27.724
Batch is simpler and exact.

1087
01:14:28.004 --> 01:14:31.716
Streaming is fresh, but must handle late and out-of-order events.

1088
01:14:33.066 --> 01:14:34.069
Intermediate.

1089
01:14:34.349 --> 01:14:35.458
Question 5.

1090
01:14:35.738 --> 01:14:39.962
How do presigned URLs work, and what are the security considerations?

1091
01:14:40.242 --> 01:14:41.607
Thirty-second answer:

1092
01:14:41.887 --> 01:14:53.386
The server signs a URL with its credentials (for S3, Signature V4): the method, bucket per key, expiry and selected headers go into an HMAC-signed string.

1093
01:14:53.666 --> 01:15:00.002
Anyone with the URL can perform exactly that operation until it expires, without credentials.

1094
01:15:00.282 --> 01:15:08.218
Keep expiries short, scope to one key, fix the content type, never log the URLs, and authorise the user before signing.

1095
01:15:09.568 --> 01:15:10.677
Question 6.

1096
01:15:10.957 --> 01:15:15.011
What goes into a CDN cache key, and how do you get a high hit ratio?

1097
01:15:15.291 --> 01:15:16.656
Thirty-second answer:

1098
01:15:16.936 --> 01:15:24.509
The key identifies which cached copy answers a request: typically the host, path and query string, plus any Vary headers.

1099
01:15:24.789 --> 01:15:26.197
For a high hit ratio:

1100
01:15:27.547 --> 01:15:28.635
Question 7.

1101
01:15:28.915 --> 01:15:32.286
How do you invalidate CDN content when a price changes?

1102
01:15:32.566 --> 01:15:33.931
Thirty-second answer:

1103
01:15:34.211 --> 01:15:46.329
Combine short TTLs with active purging by surrogate key (tag responses with sku-7 and purge the tag when sku-7 changes), and use stale-while-revalidate for smooth refreshes.

1104
01:15:46.609 --> 01:15:52.027
For static assets, never purge: version the URLs (content hash) and cache them forever.

1105
01:15:53.377 --> 01:15:54.401
Question 8.

1106
01:15:54.681 --> 01:15:59.119
Explain row versus columnar storage and why Parquet is so much smaller.

1107
01:15:59.399 --> 01:16:00.764
Thirty-second answer:

1108
01:16:01.044 --> 01:16:07.785
Row storage keeps all columns of a row together, which is ideal for reading or writing whole records by key.

1109
01:16:08.065 --> 01:16:15.233
Columnar storage keeps each column's values together, which is ideal for scanning a few columns across many rows.

1110
01:16:15.513 --> 01:16:24.260
Similar values sit next to each other, so encodings (dictionary, run-length, delta) and compression (zstd, snappy) work very well.

1111
01:16:25.610 --> 01:16:26.677
Question 9.

1112
01:16:26.957 --> 01:16:29.730
How do you make a batch job restartable and correct?

1113
01:16:30.010 --> 01:16:31.375
Thirty-second answer:

1114
01:16:31.655 --> 01:16:43.239
Process in chunks, and commit each chunk's output together with a checkpoint (the last key processed) in the same transaction, so a restart resumes exactly after the last committed chunk.

1115
01:16:43.519 --> 01:16:50.090
Alternatively, make the output idempotent: recompute a whole partition (a day) and replace it atomically.

1116
01:16:50.370 --> 01:16:55.063
Also make the reader deterministic (keyset pagination on a unique sort key).

1117
01:16:56.413 --> 01:16:57.181
Senior.

1118
01:16:57.461 --> 01:16:58.507
Question 10.

1119
01:16:58.787 --> 01:17:01.432
Warehouse, lake or lakehouse: how do you choose?

1120
01:17:02.782 --> 01:17:03.913
Question 11.

1121
01:17:04.193 --> 01:17:09.761
Event time versus processing time, watermarks and late data: explain with an example.

1122
01:17:10.041 --> 01:17:11.406
Thirty-second answer:

1123
01:17:11.686 --> 01:17:16.742
Event time is when something happened (the order was placed at 19:42:10).

1124
01:17:17.022 --> 01:17:24.126
Processing time is when the stream processor saw it (19:44:30, because the phone was in a basement).

1125
01:17:24.406 --> 01:17:27.009
Windows must use event time to be correct.

1126
01:17:27.289 --> 01:17:39.235
A watermark estimates how far event time has progressed (no events before 19:42:05 are still expected), and a window fires when the watermark passes its end.

1127
01:17:39.515 --> 01:17:43.270
Late events are dropped or, with allowed lateness, update the result.

1128
01:17:44.620 --> 01:17:45.708
Question 12.

1129
01:17:45.988 --> 01:17:51.919
Design an ELT pipeline from the order service's PostgreSQL to the analytics layer.

1130
01:17:53.269 --> 01:17:54.549
Question 13.

1131
01:17:54.829 --> 01:17:58.647
What causes a thundering herd at a CDN and how is it mitigated?

1132
01:17:58.927 --> 01:18:00.293
Thirty-second answer:

1133
01:18:00.573 --> 01:18:10.493
When a popular object expires or is purged, every edge that receives requests at that moment misses simultaneously and forwards them all to the origin.

1134
01:18:10.773 --> 01:18:11.818
Mitigations:

1135
01:18:13.168 --> 01:18:14.363
Question 14.

1136
01:18:14.643 --> 01:18:19.848
How do you handle GDPR or DPDP deletion requests in a data lake?

1137
01:18:20.128 --> 01:18:21.493
Thirty-second answer:

1138
01:18:21.773 --> 01:18:28.365
Plain Parquet files are immutable, so deleting a customer means rewriting every file that contains them.

1139
01:18:28.645 --> 01:18:45.883
Use a table format with row-level deletes (Iceberg or Delta: position or equality delete files or copy-on-write), partition and cluster so a customer's data is localised, and run compaction plus snapshot expiry so deleted data is physically removed.

1140
01:18:46.163 --> 01:18:52.563
Also pseudonymise identifiers in analytical layers so most tables contain no direct PII.

1141
01:18:53.913 --> 01:18:54.809
